#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "440cb768-be67-5dd3-98c8-6a58f060245c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a0d836-c5eb-5cb5-9a9c-f4c6843eeff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+45}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343269} {:answer/id #uuid "935feaba-b58b-536d-a925-4d51a9c3eb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343270} {:answer/id #uuid "61aca6c6-ea12-5134-8a15-4892a48b3d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343271} {:answer/id #uuid "1264516d-b07d-57b1-a865-60a7d4e5ed23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343272} {:answer/id #uuid "39bb3b6f-0f49-5db8-b1a0-aab861aabceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+45}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1264516d-b07d-57b1-a865-60a7d4e5ed23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343272} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cfe3a2a-5cb5-515e-bc99-cc3e0abc75be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fb44864-edbc-5a3d-bf6d-c1269d74e67b" :question/math-academy-id "q-123143" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+49{y}^{2}=196,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{7}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $7$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=7\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }7\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08dc697e-fd67-5ae0-889b-ea81c69e2277" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2ada7c9-c32c-5053-8011-b6e32871c53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{π}\\cos 2t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177491} {:answer/id #uuid "e424c683-6a57-5af3-a0c6-471c965bd188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\cos 2t\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + 5}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177492} {:answer/id #uuid "07cf7783-2b07-5857-a22e-be7f9103d5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sin 2t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177493} {:answer/id #uuid "58d231a8-2ec9-5b8c-8c42-45e807342369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6∫_{0}^{π}\\sin 2t\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + 5}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177494} {:answer/id #uuid "7f722537-ae73-55b1-8ad0-a29ca5436b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\cos 2t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f722537-ae73-55b1-8ad0-a29ca5436b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\cos 2t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177495} :answer-field/id #uuid "6350a452-6520-5ea5-8f9a-82544f33441e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f83a1e2-579b-5a18-9def-ff09a4759ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-123193" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061629/assets/0b0f3ae9a464812957c297c09ff3f65bfe5964bdac70298e72ec3dbde2325e72.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $6{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $\\sqrt{6},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =6{cos}^{2}⁡t-6{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =6({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ed80030-2c39-5b66-a3c5-fb1a8428a780" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2206eca-ad6b-5b79-aebe-f93d9b64a951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343276} {:answer/id #uuid "aae87c32-f618-5905-9157-ef9f1eca8890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343277} {:answer/id #uuid "6c621dd3-44c2-586c-be78-ca2e601af918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343278} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c883d7-38ab-5e43-8245-595c2f19b186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343279} {:answer/id #uuid "b284e378-a995-5ee9-8b85-8426ced4b0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b284e378-a995-5ee9-8b85-8426ced4b0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343280} :answer-field/id #uuid "c847da87-e5d3-5283-920b-4525d5e4edc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb79c2b6-b47f-5c40-8040-0229364971fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-123212" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=4xy$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡t+2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=4xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=8\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{2+2{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{2+2{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2+2{sin}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{2+2{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{2}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08dc697e-fd67-5ae0-889b-ea81c69e2277" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4476d268-1656-5669-9920-2406866977b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343283} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbd637a-88c0-5ac6-a745-ac61e313b6b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343284} {:answer/id #uuid "e11eaeda-0d45-5153-bead-5d3b483fd023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343285} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6cab0b-7f1d-5e4e-9cbb-45195904bd97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343286} {:answer/id #uuid "5c481085-79c4-5b6f-9ae9-e7525fbe6d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e11eaeda-0d45-5153-bead-5d3b483fd023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343285} :answer-field/id #uuid "14641101-17e2-598d-a733-14b7bdd14240" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c4c7b88-1836-58e7-ae69-c1739e3c9063" :question/math-academy-id "q-123303" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061629/assets/9684a8beb9981ac024d56d7467672b66c73eeb90c8dcaae11e61c0686dc1fcde.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(4x-2y)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is three-quarters of the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $4,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{cos}^{2}⁡t+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=4x-2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-2(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(4x-2y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ed80030-2c39-5b66-a3c5-fb1a8428a780" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd4b2403-5085-5fed-baac-8e302d3e9e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178578} {:answer/id #uuid "eead44d3-e7d3-5b29-a0b2-4394bf6ef615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178579} {:answer/id #uuid "f2df61f6-39f0-51f5-840f-0669b6c353a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178580} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7af2e6-c519-5122-935c-2ae060c0c5e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178581} {:answer/id #uuid "78ba19ff-b026-5a39-95a4-536a3b1b82d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2df61f6-39f0-51f5-840f-0669b6c353a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178580} :answer-field/id #uuid "88113a04-2db8-5a1d-be8b-19cb76a2547e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d6d33094-0194-5a44-b10e-00c00343eeb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-41145" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061629/assets/78f8ca3e70afb8377748a18d7024ba297e330add59550a41a87025a0145b56bb.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}9xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$  traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(8-1) \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
